WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:02.700 --> 00:03.370
My old house?

3
00:03.680 --> 00:05.580
Did I really travel back in time?

4
00:06.020 --> 00:08.700
(To have other things to think about when I die,)

5
00:09.210 --> 00:11.490
this Jiraphat will become a good boy.

6
00:16.030 --> 00:17.540
You can’t smoke in school.

7
00:17.660 --> 00:18.500
You jackass!

8
00:18.930 --> 00:20.400
Ji, catch this.

9
00:26.880 --> 00:27.620
Jiraphat.

10
00:28.440 --> 00:30.400
Why are you staring at me like that?

11
00:31.040 --> 00:33.490
Wait. What date and year is it?

12
00:33.510 --> 00:34.900
Why are you asking?

13
00:35.410 --> 00:37.620
May 31, 2010.

14
02:17.200 --> 02:19.370
NARONGRIT BOXING GYM

15
02:32.560 --> 02:34.060
Are you following me?

16
02:36.070 --> 02:37.820
No, I’m not. Get over yourself.

17
02:37.960 --> 02:39.100
You go to the boxing gym?

18
02:40.730 --> 02:41.980
Yes, this is my house.

19
02:43.640 --> 02:45.540
But the owner is related to Mr. Narong.

20
02:45.810 --> 02:46.500
Mmm.

21
02:46.950 --> 02:48.400
So you and Mr. Narong are…

22
02:48.710 --> 02:49.800
He’s my uncle.

23
02:52.920 --> 02:54.700
But your family names are different.

24
02:55.760 --> 02:58.660
He’s from my mom’s side, so we have different names.

25
02:59.780 --> 03:01.060
What’s with these bruises on your body?

26
03:01.250 --> 03:02.200
From boxing training.

27
03:02.520 --> 03:05.950
You think I always get into fist fights like people say?

28
03:06.760 --> 03:08.840
So who knows about this?

29
03:08.870 --> 03:11.140
Apart from you, just two people.

30
03:12.260 --> 03:13.100
-Lita. -Lita.

31
03:14.360 --> 03:15.340
And who’s the other one?

32
03:16.340 --> 03:17.260
The president.

33
03:18.410 --> 03:19.300
The president?

34
03:19.580 --> 03:20.730
Yeah, why?

35
03:22.630 --> 03:24.400
Nothing. I should go.

36
03:27.640 --> 03:29.770
NARONGRIT BOXING GYM

37
03:34.700 --> 03:35.700
I’m home, mom.

38
03:36.090 --> 03:38.380
You’re back? How was your day?

39
03:42.580 --> 03:44.100
I’ve made a new friend today.

40
03:44.470 --> 03:45.310
His name’s Kong.

41
03:45.350 --> 03:47.380
He’s related to the owner of the boxing gym around the corner.

42
03:48.300 --> 03:50.040
Why don’t you invite him here sometime?

43
03:50.080 --> 03:51.500
Ty, too.

44
03:53.600 --> 03:54.700
Okay, I will.

45
04:07.990 --> 04:10.400
Ked, Pim, what is everyone freaking out about?

46
04:10.990 --> 04:13.300
It’s Ms. Suda’s homework, of course.

47
04:14.420 --> 04:16.800
Seriously, who would finish it in time?

48
04:17.290 --> 04:18.430
She barely taught us anything.

49
04:18.470 --> 04:20.050
I could only do five items.

50
04:20.660 --> 04:23.060
Yeah, I could only do five of them, too.

51
04:23.230 --> 04:24.690
What? What do you mean five?

52
04:24.710 --> 04:25.450
Eh?

53
04:25.500 --> 04:26.790
You’ve just copied mine.

54
04:26.820 --> 04:28.310
Well, if I hadn’t left the school club so late,

55
04:28.340 --> 04:30.200
I would have finished five items, too.

56
04:30.890 --> 04:32.160
What about you, Ji?

57
04:32.200 --> 04:33.660
Do you want to copy mine?

58
04:34.600 --> 04:36.180
No, thanks. I finished it.

59
04:36.560 --> 04:38.000
-Huh? -Huh?

60
04:38.710 --> 04:39.700
You finished?

61
04:39.850 --> 04:40.380
Mmm.

62
04:40.520 --> 04:41.700
All ten items?

63
04:42.620 --> 04:43.340
Mmm.

64
04:51.470 --> 04:53.540
Guys, Ji’s finished his homework.

65
05:00.610 --> 05:02.540
You could do all of them?

66
05:03.020 --> 05:06.580
(Of course, I could. I studied a program that requires math scores.)

67
05:06.620 --> 05:10.420
(I work in finance, and I’ve studied all this material before.)

68
05:10.720 --> 05:12.950
I did what I could, but I’m not so sure.

69
05:17.870 --> 05:19.810
-Give it to me when you’re finished, okay? -Yeah.

70
05:19.950 --> 05:20.700
You’re late.

71
05:20.990 --> 05:23.340
My dad forgot something so we had to turn back.

72
05:23.690 --> 05:26.340
And it’s been raining so heavily. I’m all soaked.

73
05:30.100 --> 05:32.700
What is everyone doing?

74
05:33.120 --> 05:34.660
Ms. Suda’s ten-item homework.

75
05:35.040 --> 05:36.500
Have you finished it?

76
05:38.760 --> 05:39.900
Who’s Suda?

77
05:44.900 --> 05:46.090
Everyone,

78
05:46.350 --> 05:49.260
I have two important announcements to make today.

79
05:49.710 --> 05:54.100
First, there’s been a small change to this year’s field trip.

80
05:54.640 --> 05:56.100
We’re going to…

81
05:58.960 --> 06:02.700
Uh, could everyone please listen to me?

82
06:04.210 --> 06:05.500
Listen up, guys.

83
06:06.270 --> 06:08.700
I just need three minutes of your time, okay?

84
06:09.870 --> 06:14.810
Okay, first, there’s been a small change to this year’s field trip.

85
06:14.970 --> 06:16.380
Instead of a day trip,

86
06:16.420 --> 06:18.690
we’ll be staying overnight for two days and one night.

87
06:18.730 --> 06:20.620
This is because we’ve received support from…

88
06:20.660 --> 06:22.390
Uh, okay.

89
06:22.430 --> 06:25.900
Let’s go over the details at the meeting instead.

90
06:26.340 --> 06:28.500
Now, for the second announcement,

91
06:28.910 --> 06:30.980
the upcoming Wai Khru ceremony.

92
06:31.280 --> 06:34.980
Each class must select five students to make the ceremonial offering trays.

93
06:35.180 --> 06:37.100
Our Class 7 already has three volunteers:

94
06:37.560 --> 06:40.100
Kedsarin, Busaba, and Phappim.

95
06:40.220 --> 06:43.800
I need two more. Anyone willing to volunteer?

96
06:46.820 --> 06:50.340
Oh, wow! We have so many volunteers.

97
06:50.640 --> 06:53.000
The offering trays don’t have to be that beautiful.

98
06:53.020 --> 06:54.500
Just make sure you finish them.

99
06:54.540 --> 06:56.800
As for the materials, since Busaba’s family runs a flower shop…

100
06:56.830 --> 06:59.380
-Hey. -Sir, Jiraphat wants to do it.

101
06:59.900 --> 07:00.740
Really, Jiraphat?

102
07:05.050 --> 07:06.740
-I guess, sir. -Terrific.

103
07:06.940 --> 07:11.660
Kedsarin, can you please add Jiraphat and Tyler to the list?

104
07:12.100 --> 07:14.300
Huh? Me, too?!

105
07:14.520 --> 07:16.420
Why do I have to do it?

106
07:16.580 --> 07:18.660
Well, didn’t you raise your hand?

107
07:18.980 --> 07:20.990
He did. Tyler did raise his hand.

108
07:21.120 --> 07:22.290
Okay, we’ve got enough people.

109
07:22.310 --> 07:23.510
Let’s give them a hand.

110
07:24.500 --> 07:25.620
Thank you.

111
07:32.520 --> 07:33.940
Attention, students.

112
07:34.450 --> 07:36.700
Good morning.

113
07:37.870 --> 07:38.800
Now let’s see.

114
07:39.480 --> 07:42.340
How well have you all prepared for my class?

115
07:42.750 --> 07:45.900
I’ll randomly call on students to do the homework I assigned.

116
07:46.180 --> 07:50.480
(No wonder I used to skip almost every math class in my past life.)

117
07:50.570 --> 07:52.260
Jiraphat, number 7.

118
07:52.850 --> 07:54.200
Let me see your notebook.

119
08:08.500 --> 08:10.140
You did all this yourself, Jiraphat?

120
08:11.340 --> 08:12.060
Yes, ma’am.

121
08:12.330 --> 08:13.940
I haven’t taught these 10 problems yet.

122
08:13.980 --> 08:15.340
How did you get them all right?

123
08:16.170 --> 08:18.140
Did you copy the answers from another class?

124
08:19.080 --> 08:20.260
No, I didn’t.

125
08:26.180 --> 08:29.300
Then show me how you solve problem number 10.

126
09:08.760 --> 09:10.500
I don’t believe you can do this yourself.

127
09:11.890 --> 09:13.420
Did you copy it from someone else?

128
09:16.590 --> 09:18.270
Who would I copy from?

129
09:18.450 --> 09:21.340
Then who can vouch for you, huh?

130
09:26.450 --> 09:27.940
Ji did it himself, ma’am.

131
09:28.590 --> 09:29.940
I copied from him.

132
09:32.980 --> 09:34.740
I copied from Jiraphat, too.

133
09:36.070 --> 09:36.980
Me, too.

134
09:37.920 --> 09:39.400
Jiraphat did it all by himself.

135
09:39.630 --> 09:40.450
I couldn’t do it.

136
09:40.590 --> 09:42.300
-Me, neither. -Me, neither.

137
09:42.750 --> 09:43.540
Me, neither.

138
09:44.410 --> 09:45.200
Me, neither.

139
09:49.000 --> 09:50.660
Since you all cheated,

140
09:50.700 --> 09:52.500
there’s no point in me teaching you anymore.

141
10:03.190 --> 10:04.600
Don’t mind her, Ji.

142
10:04.940 --> 10:07.740
I think she wants to finish her own work anyway.

143
10:08.020 --> 10:10.980
If it weren’t you, she’d probably pick on another student anyway.

144
10:13.300 --> 10:15.380
Right. The audacity.

145
10:15.690 --> 10:18.100
She didn’t teach us anything. Who could have done it?

146
10:20.700 --> 10:24.860
Ji, you looked so cool while solving that problem.

147
10:27.640 --> 10:30.260
Why don’t you just teach us how to solve the problems?

148
10:30.970 --> 10:35.000
But if Ms. Suda finds out, won’t she be mad at us?

149
10:35.290 --> 10:37.690
How can a teacher you only see a few times a week

150
10:37.950 --> 10:40.700
compare to classmates you spend the whole year with?

151
10:45.780 --> 10:48.820
Okay, let’s start with the basics, then.

152
10:49.260 --> 10:52.200
You can refer to your textbooks as we go.

153
10:52.540 --> 10:55.700
(If I hadn’t taken everyone for granted 13 years ago,)

154
10:55.820 --> 10:58.300
(I might not be living with regrets at 30.)

155
10:58.850 --> 11:02.370
(No one should look down on Class 5/7.)

156
11:02.610 --> 11:03.610
(Not even me.)

157
11:07.520 --> 11:10.040
ATIWITCH RATTANAWATTHANA, CLASS PRESIDENT

158
11:10.080 --> 11:11.800
What should we do about Ms. Suda?

159
11:12.020 --> 11:13.520
If this keeps up,

160
11:13.560 --> 11:16.000
the Science-Math and Arts-Language classes are bound to fight.

161
11:16.200 --> 11:18.540
What’s the problem? She teaches pretty well.

162
11:18.820 --> 11:21.700
She did a great job teaching matrices.

163
11:21.800 --> 11:25.220
You only say that because she favors you.

164
11:25.390 --> 11:28.820
Today, she walked out of my class before it was even half over.

165
11:29.070 --> 11:33.780
Luckily, Jiraphat was able to teach us instead.

166
11:38.090 --> 11:40.800
Since we can’t agree, what should we do, President?

167
11:50.040 --> 11:51.510
SCHOOL CO-OP

168
11:51.550 --> 11:54.660
Oh, you saved us today, Ji.

169
11:55.710 --> 11:57.830
That’s right. I’m still shocked.

170
11:58.310 --> 12:01.020
In grade 10, you didn’t pay this much attention in class.

171
12:01.370 --> 12:06.620
What turned that unbothered Jiraphat into Jiraphat The Good Boy?

172
12:07.090 --> 12:08.650
Would you stop calling me that?

173
12:08.670 --> 12:09.250
Oh.

174
12:09.320 --> 12:11.300
Why, Ji? I think it’s kind of cute.

175
12:11.740 --> 12:12.820
Jiraphat The Good Boy.

176
12:13.150 --> 12:14.260
Tyler The Good Boy.

177
12:17.420 --> 12:18.820
Kongpob The Good Boy!

178
12:22.930 --> 12:24.860
-Did you spill your rice? -Just one.

179
12:25.470 --> 12:27.850
Where is it now? Every rice counts.

180
12:28.000 --> 12:29.240
Come and sit, my friend, Kong.

181
12:33.750 --> 12:36.000
That’s it for today. Class dismissed, everyone.

182
12:36.040 --> 12:38.180
Thank you, ma’am.

183
12:40.860 --> 12:43.100
Hey, that evaluation form you asked me to make,

184
12:43.570 --> 12:44.500
I’ve finished it.

185
12:45.870 --> 12:48.300
And I’ve talked to some of the students.

186
12:48.600 --> 12:51.100
We should have enough evidence to present to the school.

187
12:51.780 --> 12:54.900
But why did you ask me to conduct the interviews?

188
12:55.150 --> 12:56.820
I’m not a journalist.

189
12:58.220 --> 13:00.700
Come on. Think of it as aearly internship.

190
13:01.510 --> 13:02.250
What?

191
13:02.800 --> 13:03.900
How would you know about that?

192
13:11.650 --> 13:13.300
Hey, you’re leaving?

193
13:13.930 --> 13:15.600
-Mmm. -Why leave so early today?

194
13:16.600 --> 13:18.400
I have to talk to my dad about something.

195
13:18.790 --> 13:19.650
See you later.

196
13:27.550 --> 13:29.000
I signed up to make the offering tray.

197
13:30.120 --> 13:32.300
So you can go home with us?

198
13:32.600 --> 13:33.600
Of course not.

199
13:34.090 --> 13:35.540
Don't act nonchalant.

200
13:36.050 --> 13:38.540
It's to celebrate our unbreakable friendship…

201
13:38.890 --> 13:41.300
Uncle, can I have a Power Rangers pancake?

202
13:41.520 --> 13:42.300
I want one, too.

203
13:42.330 --> 13:43.660
What? I don’t know what that is!

204
13:46.330 --> 13:49.400
Then I’ll have three custard pancakes.

205
13:50.020 --> 13:51.550
And minced pork pancakes, too.

206
13:51.580 --> 13:52.900
That I can do.

207
14:11.940 --> 14:12.900
I’m home.

208
14:14.270 --> 14:16.200
You’re back? How was school?

209
14:16.480 --> 14:17.460
It was fine.

210
14:20.030 --> 14:22.200
Mom, well…

211
14:25.170 --> 14:25.950
Come in.

212
14:28.530 --> 14:29.260
Good evening.

213
14:32.370 --> 14:33.400
This is Tyler.

214
14:34.470 --> 14:36.300
And this is Kongpob.

215
14:36.690 --> 14:39.900
Oh, my. Why didn’t you tell me you invited your friends home?

216
14:40.070 --> 14:41.800
I would have prepared more food.

217
14:42.160 --> 14:45.000
That’s alright, mom. They’re only here for a moment.

218
14:45.890 --> 14:47.830
My customer’s coming to pick up the desserts.

219
14:47.850 --> 14:49.600
Can you give them these?

220
14:49.990 --> 14:50.940
Where are you going?

221
14:51.130 --> 14:52.940
I’ll go make more food.

222
14:53.150 --> 14:54.580
Make yourselves at home.

223
14:54.880 --> 14:55.680
-Thank you. -Thank you.

224
14:55.710 --> 14:56.730
Do you need a hand?

225
14:56.960 --> 14:58.820
It’s okay. You hang out with your friends.

226
15:00.760 --> 15:02.500
Have you ever tried Thong Yot?

227
15:02.830 --> 15:05.100
Are we disturbing your mom?

228
15:05.930 --> 15:08.700
No, she’s just surprised.

229
15:09.040 --> 15:10.670
‘Cause I’ve never brought any friends home.

230
15:14.130 --> 15:18.340
Oh, hi, kitty. You’re so cute.

231
15:18.440 --> 15:19.460
Kitty, kitty, kitty.

232
15:20.240 --> 15:21.460
Kitty, kitty, kitty.

233
15:26.840 --> 15:29.900
Here, my mom made some hot cocoa.

234
15:30.130 --> 15:31.580
Her special recipe.

235
15:32.840 --> 15:33.580
Try it.

236
15:34.040 --> 15:35.000
Thank you.

237
15:35.330 --> 15:36.700
-Kongpob. -Here.

238
15:43.730 --> 15:44.550
Hmm.

239
15:45.140 --> 15:46.060
So delicious, Ji.

240
15:46.990 --> 15:48.300
One cup is not enough.

241
15:48.420 --> 15:50.300
Yeah. Better than what those cafés make.

242
15:50.590 --> 15:51.330
True.

243
15:57.050 --> 15:57.800
What?

244
15:58.550 --> 15:59.460
My butt hurts.

245
15:59.970 --> 16:00.700
Yeah.

246
16:01.600 --> 16:06.140
Yeah, I’ve asked my parents many times to buy couches instead.

247
16:08.740 --> 16:10.800
Tyler, do you have a couch at home?

248
16:11.370 --> 16:12.200
Of course, Ji.

249
16:12.440 --> 16:15.900
I have a huge comfortable couch.

250
16:16.230 --> 16:17.780
You can even sleep in it.

251
16:18.080 --> 16:21.000
And it’s not so hard like this one, Ji.

252
16:22.050 --> 16:22.780
Tyler.

253
16:27.030 --> 16:28.300
Ji, I have a question.

254
16:28.590 --> 16:29.200
Hmm?

255
16:29.640 --> 16:31.220
What's bigger than a whale?

256
16:31.350 --> 16:32.700
A deep-fried whale.

257
16:32.720 --> 16:34.550
Then what's bigger than a deep-fried whale?

258
16:34.590 --> 16:35.860
The pan used to deep-fry a whale.

259
16:35.890 --> 16:37.860
Ugh, how about this?

260
16:38.170 --> 16:39.200
Name a colorful jelly.

261
16:39.240 --> 16:39.830
Pipo.

262
16:39.860 --> 16:41.490
Ugh, name a jelly with one color.

263
16:41.530 --> 16:42.380
One Pipo.

264
16:42.410 --> 16:43.570
Name a jelly with no color.

265
16:43.600 --> 16:45.000
No Pipo.

266
16:45.600 --> 16:46.840
How did you know that, Ji?

267
16:46.880 --> 16:48.370
You cheated, didn’t you?

268
16:48.800 --> 16:50.650
Is this what you two usually do?

269
16:50.690 --> 16:52.780
Here, I’ve got another question.

270
16:52.810 --> 16:54.500
This one is challenging.

271
16:54.880 --> 17:01.100
Which part of your body is long, hard, and hairy?

272
17:02.200 --> 17:03.100
-Tyler! -Tyler!

273
17:03.240 --> 17:05.010
It’s your leg, of course.

274
17:05.450 --> 17:07.270
That one was hard, right?

275
17:07.310 --> 17:09.360
No one knew the answer.

276
17:09.660 --> 17:12.800
Do you even know that question was a dirty joke?

277
17:14.120 --> 17:15.420
What? I don’t know.

278
17:16.800 --> 17:17.550
Yeah.

279
17:19.780 --> 17:22.060
Hey, Kong, my friend. Come here.

280
17:22.480 --> 17:23.400
I have a question.

281
17:23.840 --> 17:25.060
This one is so cool.

282
17:27.290 --> 17:29.020
The question is

283
17:29.340 --> 17:35.260
Which part of your body is long, hard, and hairy?

284
17:36.050 --> 17:37.000
Hmm.

285
17:37.770 --> 17:38.780
I don’t know. What?

286
17:39.060 --> 17:40.780
Your leg, of course.

287
17:41.110 --> 17:42.460
Ah, right.

288
17:42.870 --> 17:44.760
See? My question was great, wasn’t it?

289
17:44.790 --> 17:46.400
Yeah, yeah. Do you have more?

290
17:46.430 --> 17:47.740
I certainly do.

291
17:48.590 --> 17:52.450
A billionaire is very superstitious…

292
17:52.490 --> 17:54.400
We’re here, we’re here.

293
17:55.160 --> 17:56.900
Let’s get to work, everyone.

294
17:57.760 --> 17:58.900
-Thank you. -Thank you.

295
17:58.930 --> 18:00.820
-Pass that around. -I wasn’t finished.

296
18:00.850 --> 18:01.820
Pass that around.

297
18:02.820 --> 18:04.200
Let’s do this.

298
18:04.330 --> 18:07.780
Ji, what’s this?

299
18:08.190 --> 18:10.000
-A crown flower. -A crown flower.

300
18:13.720 --> 18:15.100
What about this?

301
18:15.910 --> 18:17.300
Bermuda grass.

302
18:17.800 --> 18:18.980
Bermuda grass.

303
18:19.730 --> 18:23.460
And what are these fruits for?

304
18:23.620 --> 18:24.460
Can I eat them?

305
18:24.530 --> 18:26.900
Hey, they’re for the offering trays.

306
18:27.290 --> 18:28.220
Offering trays?

307
18:28.260 --> 18:28.980
Mmm.

308
18:29.210 --> 18:31.300
Mr. Tin said that the trays don’t have to look beautiful.

309
18:31.340 --> 18:34.260
So we’re using these fruits as materials.

310
18:34.730 --> 18:37.550
We can eat them after the ceremony. No food waste.

311
18:37.590 --> 18:38.800
Mmm, right, right.

312
18:41.050 --> 18:42.980
Tyler, what are you chewing?

313
18:43.510 --> 18:44.260
Popcorn.

314
18:45.450 --> 18:46.750
This popcorn tastes weird.

315
18:46.780 --> 18:48.700
It’s bland and stale, too.

316
18:49.010 --> 18:50.770
Don’t buy this again.

317
18:51.190 --> 18:52.500
That is not popcorn.

318
18:53.150 --> 18:54.500
It’s popped rice.

319
18:54.850 --> 18:56.860
It’s for the decorations.

320
18:59.380 --> 19:01.780
Eww. Eww, eww, eww.

321
19:02.400 --> 19:03.650
Disgusting.

322
19:03.700 --> 19:05.860
That’s disgusting. Why did you eat that?

323
19:05.890 --> 19:09.220
-It doesn’t taste good at all. -Yeah, ‘cause it’s not popcorn.

324
19:09.560 --> 19:12.650
RECORD OF MISCONDUCT

325
19:16.560 --> 19:18.890
LIST OF STUDENTS ABSENT FROM MATH CLASS MS. SUDA WIRIYAPHONG

326
19:20.320 --> 19:23.810
JIRAPHAT JAROEWONG, GRADE 10, CLASS 7, 10 ABSENCES

327
19:26.610 --> 19:29.000
President, Ms. Suda wants to see you.

328
19:29.480 --> 19:33.980
STUDENT COUNCIL

329
19:34.110 --> 19:37.540
I heard you’re leading the effort to file a complaint against me.

330
19:38.050 --> 19:39.950
There must be some misunderstanding.

331
19:48.090 --> 19:49.300
That is so disappointing.

332
19:49.660 --> 19:52.000
You used to be such an obedient student.

333
19:52.430 --> 19:53.980
I wonder what made you this way.

334
19:56.480 --> 20:00.380
You’ve been hanging around with the students in Class 7, right?

335
20:00.400 --> 20:01.200
Ma’am.

336
20:06.420 --> 20:09.200
You’re the reason I have to do this.

337
20:13.740 --> 20:14.740
What do you mean?

338
20:17.680 --> 20:19.740
If you had done your job properly,

339
20:20.630 --> 20:21.930
treated everyone fairly,

340
20:22.620 --> 20:24.290
and upheld professional teaching ethics,

341
20:25.300 --> 20:27.100
we wouldn’t have had to do this.

342
20:31.870 --> 20:33.340
What do you want me to do?

343
20:34.090 --> 20:35.700
Apologize to those students?

344
20:37.450 --> 20:38.620
That would be great, ma’am.

345
20:41.160 --> 20:43.220
It seems we’re not getting anywhere.

346
20:46.000 --> 20:47.300
A good student like you…

347
20:47.970 --> 20:50.300
wouldn’t want me to call your parents in, would you?

348
20:56.520 --> 20:57.490
Go ahead and call them.

349
20:59.700 --> 21:00.900
Actually,…

350
21:02.220 --> 21:04.500
my dad wants to have a word with you, too.

351
21:12.680 --> 21:14.890
DON’T LET TYLER EAT ANYTHING

352
21:14.910 --> 21:15.800
Wow.

353
21:16.630 --> 21:19.000
Ji’s part looks flawless.

354
21:19.740 --> 21:20.780
It’s not that good.

355
21:20.990 --> 21:22.030
And Ty’s part…

356
21:22.170 --> 21:24.220
How’s that? It’s good, right?

357
21:24.370 --> 21:26.060
Took me so long to perfect this.

358
21:28.460 --> 21:29.060
Looks great.

359
21:30.190 --> 21:32.310
Jiraphat seems like someone we can count on.

360
21:32.340 --> 21:33.300
I know, right?

361
21:33.570 --> 21:36.250
Since Ji arrived at school early on the first day of the semester,

362
21:36.290 --> 21:39.600
I could tell that Jiraphat had changed a lot.

363
21:39.800 --> 21:43.420
-Please. You can’t be serious. -Why?

364
21:43.560 --> 21:46.000
You thought I was late for school, didn’t you?

365
21:46.030 --> 21:46.780
See?

366
21:46.990 --> 21:50.200
The old Jiraphat wouldn’t talk back like that.

367
21:51.820 --> 21:52.600
Ouch!

368
21:54.570 --> 21:55.500
What happened?

369
21:56.380 --> 21:58.300
Nothing.

370
21:58.670 --> 21:59.710
Ty!

371
22:00.230 --> 22:03.000
Finish this or you don’t get to take a break.

372
22:05.240 --> 22:06.020
Listen to this.

373
22:06.290 --> 22:06.780
Yeah.

374
22:06.880 --> 22:10.700
Yesterday, Lita told me the president was a psychic.

375
22:11.210 --> 22:16.200
He asked Lita to investigate something but she refused.

376
22:16.460 --> 22:20.500
Then he said, “Think of it as an early internship.”

377
22:22.020 --> 22:23.500
How does that make him a psychic?

378
22:23.950 --> 22:27.100
Everyone knows Lita wanted to study engineering.

379
22:27.410 --> 22:30.140
She only changed her mind and decided to study communications recently.

380
22:30.500 --> 22:32.620
And I’m the only one who knows.

381
22:35.080 --> 22:37.940
(I'm so glad you've agreed to be interviewed on our show.)

382
22:37.960 --> 22:40.090
(It's been a long time. How have you been?)

383
22:40.120 --> 22:41.270
(I'm happy to be here, too.)

384
22:41.300 --> 22:43.130
(It's been a long time since we last met.)

385
22:43.140 --> 22:45.180
(It's an honor to be invited here today.)

386
22:46.810 --> 22:48.180
You're overthinking it.

387
22:48.730 --> 22:50.470
With how much she knows about other people's business,

388
22:50.510 --> 22:52.600
even I could tell she was meant to be a journalist.

389
22:53.040 --> 22:56.300
I was more surprised she said she wanted to study engineering.

390
22:57.920 --> 22:59.380
Speaking of the president,

391
22:59.420 --> 23:04.000
I’m thinking about his old nickname back in middle school: Ruth the Sweet Tooth.

392
23:04.930 --> 23:06.000
Ruth the Sweet Tooth?

393
23:06.500 --> 23:07.800
Wow.

394
23:08.270 --> 23:10.380
Since you threw the volleyball in his face,

395
23:10.420 --> 23:12.020
you seem to be interested in him.

396
23:13.950 --> 23:15.540
No. I’m just curious.

397
23:16.770 --> 23:17.500
Mmm.

398
23:18.190 --> 23:19.600
Back in middle school,

399
23:19.900 --> 23:24.650
Pim, Lita, the president, Tawan, and I were in the same class.

400
23:24.680 --> 23:26.250
GRADE 7, CLASS 1

401
23:30.600 --> 23:32.620
Aren’t you coming down to the cafeteria for lunch with us?

402
23:35.240 --> 23:37.740
Wow, those look so good. Can I have some?

403
23:38.790 --> 23:39.500
No.

404
23:40.170 --> 23:41.800
Hey, let me have some.

405
23:42.650 --> 23:45.000
-Let me try. Let me try. -No.

406
23:45.380 --> 23:46.350
No.

407
23:48.360 --> 23:50.480
After that, he never brought his lunch box to school again.

408
23:50.520 --> 23:53.430
But Tawan shouldn't have teased Ruth

409
23:53.470 --> 23:55.400
just because he wouldn't share his desserts.

410
23:55.920 --> 24:00.400
He went from Ruth the Kindhearted to President Ruth the Stern.

411
24:01.500 --> 24:02.230
Ouch!

412
24:02.540 --> 24:03.580
-Stop! -Stop!

413
24:03.780 --> 24:05.000
Not the same trick.

414
24:05.570 --> 24:07.700
You think we would believe you?

415
24:07.720 --> 24:08.380
Sh*t!

416
24:08.420 --> 24:09.380
Oh, Ji.

417
24:09.400 --> 24:11.000
You’re playing along this time?

418
24:12.260 --> 24:13.300
-Sh*t. -Sh*t.

419
24:15.070 --> 24:16.090
Ji, I’m hurt!

420
24:16.350 --> 24:19.550
If I die, tell my dad I love him.

421
24:19.860 --> 24:21.380
I love you, Dad.

422
24:24.030 --> 24:25.900
Ouch! Ow, ow, ow! Please, that hurts.

423
24:25.940 --> 24:27.380
Please go easy on me.

424
24:27.420 --> 24:29.000
-That hurts. -Tyler.

425
24:29.500 --> 24:31.110
I’ll go back and help our friends.

426
24:32.630 --> 24:34.870
Oww! That hurts.

427
24:34.910 --> 24:36.500
Stay still.

428
24:36.800 --> 24:37.710
Ahh!

429
25:14.700 --> 25:16.200
Hey, come here.

430
25:16.600 --> 25:17.660
I have something big.

431
25:18.660 --> 25:20.760
-Something big? -Don’t just stand there. Come here.

432
25:22.780 --> 25:24.800
It’s an emergency.

433
25:25.160 --> 25:25.900
What is it?

434
25:26.310 --> 25:27.900
It’s Ms. Suda.

435
25:29.540 --> 25:34.200
Pim and I went to the bathroom and Lita told us.

436
25:34.420 --> 25:36.420
It’s gonna blow your mind.

437
25:37.190 --> 25:39.220
Ms. Suda has been reported.

438
25:41.510 --> 25:42.100
That’s it?

439
25:42.200 --> 25:43.580
Not just that.

440
25:43.620 --> 25:45.420
She was also called to the principal’s office.

441
25:46.180 --> 25:47.700
The principal knows about this, too?

442
25:48.180 --> 25:49.700
I told you it was huge.

443
25:50.270 --> 25:52.400
The point isn’t that someone filed a complaint.

444
25:52.760 --> 25:55.860
It’s who filed it.

445
26:02.280 --> 26:05.900
The trays for Grades 10 and 12 have been brought into the auditorium.

446
26:06.340 --> 26:08.740
Only the Grade 11 trays are still incomplete.

447
26:10.660 --> 26:11.620
About Ms. Suda…

448
26:12.060 --> 26:14.540
I thought you and your dad don’t get along.

449
26:18.460 --> 26:22.820
You said, “I’d rather die than asking him for help.”

450
26:23.870 --> 26:26.370
We’re not that incompetent.

451
26:27.200 --> 26:30.380
It might take some time but we’ll finally find a way.

452
26:30.710 --> 26:32.800
You didn’t have to hurry and ask for his help.

453
26:41.220 --> 26:45.600
(I remember that after the student council filed a complaint,)

454
26:45.990 --> 26:48.900
(Ruth, the class president, was summoned by Ms. Suda.)

455
26:49.370 --> 26:50.260
(But back then,)

456
26:50.280 --> 26:51.200
STUDENT COUNCIL ROOM

457
26:51.240 --> 26:52.940
(It ended with her agreeing to give the notes)

458
26:52.980 --> 26:56.160
(she’d given the Science-Math students to the Arts-Languages students, too.)

459
26:56.340 --> 26:58.500
(But she continued to teach in the same biased way.)

460
26:59.310 --> 27:02.700
(That’s why I was surprised the principal had known about it.)

461
27:23.310 --> 27:25.200
How’s your face after getting hit?

462
27:25.260 --> 27:26.100
Sorry about that.

463
27:30.440 --> 27:31.400
It’s fine now.

464
27:39.890 --> 27:41.100
About Ms. Suda…

465
27:44.880 --> 27:47.350
What about her?

466
27:48.990 --> 27:50.100
Don’t worry.

467
27:50.710 --> 27:51.900
I’ve talked to her.

468
27:52.680 --> 27:55.380
You can attend class as usual.

469
27:56.670 --> 27:57.860
Why would you go this far for me?

470
27:58.170 --> 28:00.700
You disagree with what I did?

471
28:03.300 --> 28:04.470
It’s not that.

472
28:04.970 --> 28:06.540
Based on the attendance records,

473
28:06.970 --> 28:09.740
Arts-Languages students seem to skip class more often

474
28:09.780 --> 28:11.490
on days when they have math class.

475
28:12.340 --> 28:14.690
If I let this go, it definitely won’t end here.

476
28:16.350 --> 28:18.870
But Ms. Suda hasn’t caused any big trouble this semester.

477
28:18.900 --> 28:19.740
Jiraphat

478
28:23.920 --> 28:27.340
The fact that she abandoned your class and refused to continue teaching

479
28:27.990 --> 28:30.100
was a serious dereliction of her duty as a teacher.

480
28:36.050 --> 28:37.600
When she called me in,

481
28:39.260 --> 28:42.900
she said your class wasn’t as good academically as mine,

482
28:43.310 --> 28:45.600
so what she did was justified.

483
28:47.460 --> 28:48.460
But it wasn’t.

484
28:50.750 --> 28:52.900
Just because your class isn’t as good academically as mine

485
28:53.190 --> 28:57.260
doesn’t mean she can treat you however she wants.

486
29:01.080 --> 29:06.130
A teacher can’t just abandon their students like that.

487
29:08.890 --> 29:10.400
As student council president,

488
29:12.670 --> 29:14.400
I can’t let this continue.

489
29:29.280 --> 29:32.170
TEACHER APPRECIATION CEREMONY

490
29:50.660 --> 29:51.650
Thank you.

491
29:52.310 --> 29:53.300
Thank you so much.

492
30:16.120 --> 30:20.700
Hey, I heard Ms. Suda received a warning letter.

493
30:21.050 --> 30:24.340
She’ll probably have to stop teaching until the investigation is complete.

494
30:25.210 --> 30:27.700
Then who’s going to teach us math?

495
30:27.960 --> 30:29.400
I heard it’s going to be a new teacher.

496
30:29.590 --> 30:32.600
They’ll probably start after we get back from the field trip.

497
30:34.600 --> 30:35.820
Where should we sit?

498
30:36.570 --> 30:39.000
Hey, hey, that’s the second piece.

499
30:39.820 --> 30:40.630
Over there?

500
30:41.310 --> 30:42.200
Yeah, over there?

501
30:42.390 --> 30:43.900
Have some water.

502
30:44.670 --> 30:45.550
You want some water?

503
30:45.580 --> 30:46.680
It’s okay.

504
30:46.710 --> 30:48.000
Can I sit?

505
30:52.970 --> 30:54.790
The three core members of the student council.

506
30:54.820 --> 30:56.200
Any updates on Ms. Suda?

507
30:56.510 --> 30:58.170
She was called in by the principal,

508
30:58.210 --> 31:00.100
so the investigation will probably take a while.

509
31:00.650 --> 31:06.500
I heard you let the offering tray cut you so people would feel sorry for you.

510
31:06.830 --> 31:10.300
Class 7 always has something unexpected going on.

511
31:10.490 --> 31:12.660
Tawan, do you think being on the student council

512
31:12.680 --> 31:13.940
means you can say whatever you want?

513
31:14.110 --> 31:16.220
Hey, I was complimenting you.

514
31:16.580 --> 31:17.370
But at least

515
31:17.410 --> 31:19.600
our class’s fruit offering tray tastes good.

516
31:20.600 --> 31:22.400
-Hey. -Hey, Kongpob!

517
31:23.650 --> 31:25.100
My fried chicken.

518
31:25.140 --> 31:26.780
Hey, my fried chicken.

519
31:27.810 --> 31:28.700
Yummy.

520
31:29.730 --> 31:31.600
You have two pieces.

521
31:31.970 --> 31:36.320
-He was afraid you would be too full. -Tawan, I only had two pieces.

522
31:51.520 --> 31:52.300
Damn.

523
31:52.960 --> 31:54.000
So heavy.

524
31:58.220 --> 32:02.220
Jean and I have told you many times that this bench is hard and uncomfortable.

525
32:02.670 --> 32:06.500
But when Tyler and Kongpob complained, you immediately bought a new couch.

526
32:06.800 --> 32:12.420
Because your mom was afraid Ty and Kong wouldn’t come over anymore.

527
32:15.150 --> 32:19.400
You haven’t forgotten our annual health checkup, right, Dad?

528
32:19.480 --> 32:22.130
Your mom and I have already scheduled it. We’ll go together.

529
32:27.390 --> 32:31.340
Dad, I have a school trip at the end of this month.

530
32:31.560 --> 32:32.580
It’s overnight, too.

531
32:33.690 --> 32:34.620
Huh?

532
32:35.260 --> 32:36.800
Why are you making that face, Dad?

533
32:37.060 --> 32:38.700
I’m just going on a school trip.

534
32:38.860 --> 32:42.420
Usually, whenever the school has an activity,

535
32:42.450 --> 32:45.260
you pretend to be sick so you can stay home.

536
32:46.950 --> 32:48.260
But this is a good thing.

537
32:48.730 --> 32:52.010
Your school years are the perfect time to gain new experiences

538
32:52.040 --> 32:53.800
and make good memories,

539
32:54.430 --> 32:56.300
so you won’t have any regrets later in life.

540
32:59.490 --> 33:00.300
Yes, Dad.

541
33:00.580 --> 33:01.420
That’s good.

542
33:05.400 --> 33:07.300
Why are you carrying so much stuff today?

543
33:07.340 --> 33:09.050
We have art class today,

544
33:09.090 --> 33:10.800
and the teacher asked us to bring our own supplies.

545
33:11.350 --> 33:13.000
Hurry up, or you’ll get stuck in traffic.

546
33:13.120 --> 33:13.780
Okay, Dad.

547
33:15.450 --> 33:18.500
Here, take this new handkerchief.

548
33:19.050 --> 33:22.100
Use it to wipe your art supplies so they don’t stain your bag.

549
33:22.330 --> 33:24.700
-Okay, mom. I’ll go then. -Okay.

550
33:31.350 --> 33:33.700
Can you guess what this is?

551
33:35.150 --> 33:35.860
A cat.

552
33:35.910 --> 33:36.690
Nope.

553
33:36.740 --> 33:37.500
A bear.

554
33:38.260 --> 33:41.000
How is that a bear? This is definitely a cat.

555
33:41.090 --> 33:42.220
It’s Bear, Ji.

556
33:44.070 --> 33:45.940
Oh, my cat, Bear.

557
33:49.170 --> 33:50.100
This is cute.

558
33:51.440 --> 33:52.600
Of course.

559
33:52.760 --> 33:55.470
My dad is a famous artist, and he taught me himself.

560
33:55.890 --> 33:57.500
Isn’t your dad an engineer?

561
33:57.820 --> 33:59.660
How did he become a painter?

562
34:00.290 --> 34:02.220
My dad can do anything.

563
34:05.150 --> 34:08.000
Why haven’t you started drawing yet?

564
34:08.040 --> 34:09.340
It’s already the second period.

565
34:10.060 --> 34:11.320
There’s no need to rush.

566
34:11.350 --> 34:13.100
The teacher never said we had to turn it in class.

567
34:19.130 --> 34:21.000
I think we should find a new spot.

568
34:21.540 --> 34:23.900
Why, Ji? It’s fun right here.

569
34:24.630 --> 34:27.100
How is this fun? Don’t you see those people?

570
34:27.260 --> 34:28.840
If a volleyball…

571
34:31.070 --> 34:32.380
Ji, you okay?

572
34:32.590 --> 34:33.700
Let’s go to the medical room.

573
34:34.620 --> 34:36.490
Sorry. Are you okay?

574
34:37.450 --> 34:39.500
Oh, Ji and Ty?

575
34:40.000 --> 34:41.900
You must be fine then.

576
34:42.010 --> 34:45.030
Hey, why would you say that?

577
34:45.070 --> 34:48.670
Didn’t you throw a volleyball at the president’s face?

578
34:48.940 --> 34:50.000
Let’s call this even.

579
34:50.970 --> 34:52.300
Where’s Ruth?

580
34:53.850 --> 34:54.800
What the hell?

581
34:55.080 --> 34:58.340
The cute vice president’s here and you’re asking for Ruth?

582
34:58.470 --> 35:00.460
Of course he’s asking about someone who’s not here.

583
35:00.630 --> 35:03.780
Why would he ask about you?

584
35:04.230 --> 35:05.300
You’re ganging up on me.

585
35:06.720 --> 35:07.930
The president will be fine.

586
35:08.100 --> 35:09.940
He was just called in to discuss some competition

587
35:09.960 --> 35:11.100
in the Science Department.

588
35:13.140 --> 35:14.100
Again?

589
35:14.790 --> 35:17.700
You seem to be more upset than he is.

590
35:18.700 --> 35:19.700
But I get it.

591
35:19.870 --> 35:23.300
We’ve never really been able to enjoy PE class with him, either.

592
35:23.670 --> 35:26.000
It’s supposed to be our only chance to relax,

593
35:26.600 --> 35:27.940
yet he keeps getting called away.

594
35:30.860 --> 35:34.300
This is just like a sitcom I used to watch.

595
35:34.650 --> 35:35.660
What sitcom?

596
35:36.170 --> 35:38.060
Those characters who have the same name.

597
35:38.250 --> 35:39.100
Mr. Ruth.

598
35:39.810 --> 35:40.700
Security Ruth.

599
35:41.030 --> 35:41.700
Auntie Ruth.

600
35:42.320 --> 35:44.740
And this is… President Ruth.

601
35:44.850 --> 35:45.660
My friend Ruth.

602
35:45.930 --> 35:47.000
Student Ruth.

603
35:47.340 --> 35:49.820
Hey, Wan. What’s taking you so long?

604
35:50.000 --> 35:53.100
If you were in such a hurry, why didn’t you come get it yourself?

605
35:53.820 --> 35:54.600
Ugh.

606
35:55.290 --> 35:56.200
See you.

607
35:59.290 --> 36:00.800
Hey, Ji. Where are you going?

608
36:00.980 --> 36:01.680
Away from here.

609
36:01.720 --> 36:04.190
Do you want to wait for another volleyball to hit your face?

610
36:05.310 --> 36:06.200
What the hell?

611
36:07.670 --> 36:09.100
Why are you so afraid of a volleyball?

612
36:09.360 --> 36:10.070
Ouch!

613
36:11.350 --> 36:12.200
Okay, I give up.

614
36:18.460 --> 36:20.000
Don’t you want to sit there anymore?

615
36:20.260 --> 36:22.180
No, I don’t want to get injured.

616
36:26.650 --> 36:28.770
It’s you and Ty, see? Can you tell?

617
36:29.140 --> 36:29.900
Of course.

618
36:30.380 --> 36:31.900
Wow, this is beautiful.

619
36:32.450 --> 36:35.460
Really? You’re not just saying that?

620
36:35.650 --> 36:37.100
Why would I do that?

621
36:37.650 --> 36:39.160
You drew this well in just one hour.

622
36:39.190 --> 36:41.100
If you told me you were entering an art competition, I’d believe you.

623
36:44.170 --> 36:46.300
The teacher has asked me to enter competitions before.

624
36:46.760 --> 36:50.000
But if I entered one, I’d have to stay after school to practice.

625
36:51.580 --> 36:53.010
I have tutoring classes to go to,

626
36:53.050 --> 36:54.200
so I don’t have time.

627
36:57.930 --> 37:01.380
You’re good at social studies anyway.

628
37:02.060 --> 37:03.460
You’ll get into university for sure.

629
37:05.670 --> 37:07.710
Do you want to be a fortune-teller like the president,

630
37:07.740 --> 37:09.500
the way Lita told us?

631
37:11.710 --> 37:12.690
No, that’s not it.

632
37:15.450 --> 37:17.700
Oww, Ji, Pim, can I sit with you guys?

633
37:17.850 --> 37:19.800
I’ve been hit by a volleyball many times.

634
37:20.930 --> 37:22.700
There goes my peaceful moment.

635
37:22.990 --> 37:24.800
Oh, please, Pim.

636
37:25.130 --> 37:27.650
Ked is at a basketball competition today, so I was worried you’d be lonely.

637
37:30.090 --> 37:31.500
My head hurts.

638
37:35.670 --> 37:37.210
-How’s this? -There’s a cat, too.

639
37:37.250 --> 37:37.900
A bear.

640
37:38.150 --> 37:39.900
-Oh, a bear? -Little bear.

641
37:40.000 --> 37:41.000
How is that a bear?

642
37:41.250 --> 37:42.400
His name is Bear.

643
37:44.430 --> 37:45.700
Go ahead and grab it, sweetie.

644
37:46.800 --> 37:47.900
-I’ll have… -I’ll have…

645
37:50.630 --> 37:51.500
You first.

646
37:55.050 --> 37:56.020
A water, please.

647
37:58.970 --> 38:00.140
Here's your water.

648
38:01.250 --> 38:03.900
Can I have a cube of ice?

649
38:04.220 --> 38:07.800
I got hit by a volleyball, and I think my head’s starting to swell.

650
38:09.200 --> 38:10.050
Thank you.

651
38:12.100 --> 38:13.100
It kind of hurts.

652
38:14.830 --> 38:15.600
What?

653
38:17.360 --> 38:19.000
I was hit by a volleyball.

654
38:19.480 --> 38:20.600
It kind of hurt.

655
38:28.350 --> 38:29.250
Thank you.

656
38:46.470 --> 38:47.450
Wrong side.

657
39:00.430 --> 39:02.270
Didn’t you come to get a water?

658
39:02.310 --> 39:04.100
Here’s your water.

659
39:04.590 --> 39:05.400
Here.

660
39:06.110 --> 39:08.030
Thank you. I’ll do it myself.

661
39:15.250 --> 39:16.800
I’ve never been here.

662
39:17.560 --> 39:20.420
I didn’t know there’s a haunted house in our school.

663
39:20.870 --> 39:23.130
It’s a men’s room not a haunted house.

664
39:27.360 --> 39:29.000
I’ll wash the rest.

665
39:29.280 --> 39:30.500
Please just stand there.

666
39:30.830 --> 39:32.980
Hey, I can wash it properly.

667
39:33.380 --> 39:35.500
Don’t you see how dirty that brush is?

668
39:36.030 --> 39:37.400
But you’re super clean.

669
39:37.570 --> 39:38.900
You don’t have a paint stain on you.

670
39:38.940 --> 39:41.000
I’m not that bad, look.

671
39:41.710 --> 39:43.980
Actually, it’s kind of…

672
39:45.120 --> 39:46.050
Hey, Tawan.

673
39:46.740 --> 39:47.500
Hey.

674
39:48.060 --> 39:50.060
You use this bathroom, too?

675
39:50.210 --> 39:51.740
I’ve never seen you here.

676
39:52.110 --> 39:54.220
Hey, why can we use it, too?

677
39:54.540 --> 39:56.800
Is it your personal pee spot?

678
39:56.990 --> 39:58.700
Hey, I’m not a dog.

679
39:58.990 --> 40:01.100
Hold on. I’ll be back for you.

680
40:05.570 --> 40:06.300
Hey.

681
40:08.080 --> 40:08.800
Hey.

682
40:11.280 --> 40:11.980
Ji

683
40:12.230 --> 40:13.500
Water Dragon!

684
40:14.640 --> 40:15.500
Tyler!

685
40:15.610 --> 40:17.500
How was my move?

686
40:18.640 --> 40:20.060
What are you doing? Can I join?

687
40:26.660 --> 40:29.380
Tawan, my friend. Why did you hurt me?

688
40:29.560 --> 40:30.940
You’re my enemy.

689
40:31.510 --> 40:33.820
Bring it on if you dare!

690
40:36.230 --> 40:37.100
Water Dragon!

691
40:37.130 --> 40:38.200
Boom, boom, boom.

692
40:38.820 --> 40:40.400
Hey, hey, hey.

693
40:41.670 --> 40:43.200
They hell are you doing?

694
40:44.240 --> 40:46.200
The Martial Arts Tournament.

695
40:46.780 --> 40:49.340
Would you be interested in joining?

696
40:49.550 --> 40:51.990
Indeed. I’ve long wished to test my skills against you.

697
40:52.020 --> 40:53.670
Show me what you got.

698
41:03.560 --> 41:05.020
Want to borrow my handkerchief?

699
41:07.540 --> 41:10.420
Ji, this is a fight between class 1 and class 7.

700
41:10.590 --> 41:12.420
Why are you helping an enemy?

701
41:14.600 --> 41:15.400
Hold this.

702
41:16.490 --> 41:17.200
Ha!

703
41:17.570 --> 41:18.700
-Come on -Yes!

704
41:18.990 --> 41:21.220
Get out of the way. The big guy’s here!

705
41:28.300 --> 41:29.800
Water Dragon!

706
41:30.300 --> 41:32.400
Here comes The Ass Whooper!

707
41:34.780 --> 41:35.860
Water Dragon!

708
41:42.900 --> 41:44.060
I can’t believe this.

709
41:44.350 --> 41:47.460
When I heard that Class 1 and Class 7 had fought in the old bathroom,

710
41:47.700 --> 41:49.000
I was shocked.

711
41:49.590 --> 41:52.800
How could you two get into a fight with anyone?

712
41:53.600 --> 41:54.300
Now what?

713
41:54.430 --> 41:55.890
Do you have to go to the Hell Room again?

714
41:56.090 --> 41:58.650
Mr. Narong figured we hadn’t caused any serious trouble,

715
41:58.690 --> 42:01.460
so he just made us mop the floor and wipe the sinks.

716
42:01.500 --> 42:02.600
What’s wrong with you?

717
42:02.730 --> 42:04.800
You’re going to the beach tomorrow.

718
42:05.030 --> 42:06.260
Why did you do that?

719
42:11.700 --> 42:12.380
Tyler.

720
42:13.180 --> 42:14.800
Have you seen my paintbrush cloth?

721
42:16.330 --> 42:18.800
No. Did you drop it somewhere?

722
42:21.580 --> 42:23.050
Hmm, I guess so.

723
42:32.740 --> 42:34.060
What’s that cloth?

724
42:36.140 --> 42:36.900
Oh.

725
42:37.760 --> 42:38.900
Someone dropped it.

726
42:39.330 --> 42:40.450
Give it to me then.

727
42:40.540 --> 42:43.300
I’ll put it in the lost-and-found box.

728
42:45.070 --> 42:47.400
Oh, no, I mean…

729
42:48.280 --> 42:50.000
Someone gave it to me.

730
42:59.920 --> 43:01.180
Do you wanna wipe your face?

731
43:03.470 --> 43:05.550
My mom gave it to me to clean my paintbrushes,

732
43:05.590 --> 43:06.640
but I haven’t used it.

733
43:06.680 --> 43:08.100
I promise it’s clean.

734
43:10.510 --> 43:11.400
Thanks.

735
44:19.890 --> 44:21.570
Would you be interested in joining the student council?

736
44:21.890 --> 44:23.060
I’ll have to pass.

737
44:23.460 --> 44:24.970
Won’t you regret it for the rest of your life?

738
44:27.970 --> 44:29.620
(When you keep sneaking glances at someone,)

739
44:29.660 --> 44:32.470
(unless they admire them or have a crush on them,)

740
44:33.840 --> 44:35.220
(there’s usually no reason for it.)

741
44:35.320 --> 44:38.060
If only they’re looking out of interest,

742
44:38.420 --> 44:39.500
but…

743
44:40.860 --> 44:45.140
Talking to you makes me happier than watching the sunset.
